      "code": "import Mathlib\n\nstructure WeightedGraph where\n  adj : Array (Array (Nat \u00d7 Int))\n\ndef WeightedGraph.size (g : WeightedGraph) : Nat :=\n  g.adj.size\n\ndef WeightedGraph.has_edge (g : WeightedGraph) (u v : Nat) (w : Int) : Prop :=\n  u < g.size \u2227\n  \u2203 pair, pair \u2208 g.adj.getD u #[] \u2227 pair.1 = v \u2227 pair.2 = w\n\ndef WeightedGraph.well_formed (g : WeightedGraph) : Prop :=\n  \u2200 u, u < g.size \u2192\n    -- Bounds check\n    (\u2200 pair, pair \u2208 g.adj.getD u #[] \u2192 pair.1 < g.size) \u2227\n    -- Simple graph check (matches Dafny)\n    (\u2200 p1 p2, p1 \u2208 g.adj.getD u #[] \u2192 p2 \u2208 g.adj.getD u #[] \u2192 p1.1 = p2.1 \u2192 p1 = p2)\n\nstructure MSTEdge where\n  u : Nat\n  v : Nat\n  w : Int\n\n-- 1. Validity\ndef MSTEdge.is_valid (e : MSTEdge) (g : WeightedGraph) : Prop :=\n  g.has_edge e.u e.v e.w\n\n-- 2. Connectivity for Solution Edges (Undirected View)\n-- Dafny: `edge_in_set` checks (u, v) || (v, u)\ndef edges_contain (edges : List MSTEdge) (u v : Nat) : Prop :=\n  \u2203 e, e \u2208 edges \u2227 ((e.u = u \u2227 e.v = v) \u2228 (e.u = v \u2227 e.v = u))\n\ndef path_follows_edges (edges : List MSTEdge) (p : List Nat) : Prop :=\n  p.length > 0 \u2227\n  \u2200 i, i + 1 < p.length \u2192\n    edges_contain edges (p.getD i 0) (p.getD (i + 1) 0)\n\ndef is_connected_with_edges (g : WeightedGraph) (edges : List MSTEdge) : Prop :=\n  \u2200 u v, u < g.size \u2192 v < g.size \u2192\n    \u2203 p, p.head? = some u \u2227 p.getLast? = some v \u2227 path_follows_edges edges p\n\n-- 3. Spanning Tree Definition\ndef is_spanning_tree (g : WeightedGraph) (edges : List MSTEdge) : Prop :=\n  edges.length = g.size - 1 \u2227\n  (\u2200 e, e \u2208 edges \u2192 e.is_valid g) \u2227\n  is_connected_with_edges g edges\n  -- Note: |V|-1 edges + Connected implies Acyclic (Tree)\n\ndef total_weight (edges : List MSTEdge) : Int :=\n  edges.foldl (\u03bb sum e => sum + e.w) 0\n\n-- 4. Optimality (MST)\ndef is_mst (g : WeightedGraph) (edges : List MSTEdge) : Prop :=\n  is_spanning_tree g edges \u2227\n  \u2200 other, is_spanning_tree g other \u2192 total_weight edges \u2264 total_weight other\n\n-- 5. Graph Connectivity (Precondition)\n-- Dafny uses directed edges for the graph path check: `graph_has_any_edge`\ndef path_follows_graph (g : WeightedGraph) (p : List Nat) : Prop :=\n  p.length > 0 \u2227\n  \u2200 i, i + 1 < p.length \u2192\n    \u2203 w, g.has_edge (p.getD i 0) (p.getD (i + 1) 0) w\n\ndef WeightedGraph.is_connected (g : WeightedGraph) : Prop :=\n  \u2200 u v, u < g.size \u2192 v < g.size \u2192\n    \u2203 p, p.head? = some u \u2227 p.getLast? = some v \u2227 path_follows_graph g p\n\ndef WeightedGraph.weights_bounded (g : WeightedGraph) : Prop :=\n  g.size \u2264 100000 \u2227\n  \u2200 u v w, g.has_edge u v w \u2192 w \u2265 -100000 \u2227 w \u2264 100000\n\n-- Precondition definitions\n@[reducible, simp]\ndef kruskal_mst_precond (graph : WeightedGraph) : Prop :=\n  -- !benchmark @start precond\n  graph.well_formed \u2227\n  graph.size > 0 \u2227\n  graph.is_connected \u2227\n  graph.weights_bounded\n  -- !benchmark @end precond\n\n-- !benchmark @start auxcode\n\n/-- All edges of the graph, read off the adjacency array. -/\ndef collectEdges (g : WeightedGraph) : List MSTEdge :=\n  (List.range g.size).flatMap fun u =>\n    (g.adj.getD u #[]).toList.map fun p => \u27e8u, p.1, p.2\u27e9\n\n/-- Union-find `find`, run with `fuel` steps (the node count bounds any parent chain). -/\ndef ufFind : Nat \u2192 Array Nat \u2192 Nat \u2192 Nat\n  | 0, _, x => x\n  | fuel + 1, par, x =>\n      let px := par.getD x x\n      if px = x then x else ufFind fuel par px\n\n/-- Union-find `union`: link the root of `a` under the root of `b`. -/\ndef ufUnion (par : Array Nat) (fuel a b : Nat) : Array Nat :=\n  let ra := ufFind fuel par a\n  let rb := ufFind fuel par b\n  if ra = rb then par else par.setIfInBounds ra rb\n\n/-- Main Kruskal sweep: walk the weight-sorted edges, keeping an edge exactly when\nits endpoints still lie in different components. -/\ndef kruskalLoop (fuel : Nat) : List MSTEdge \u2192 Array Nat \u2192 List MSTEdge \u2192 List MSTEdge\n  | [], _, acc => acc.reverse\n  | e :: es, par, acc =>\n      if ufFind fuel par e.u = ufFind fuel par e.v then\n        kruskalLoop fuel es par acc\n      else\n        kruskalLoop fuel es (ufUnion par fuel e.u e.v) (e :: acc)\n\n/-- Kruskal's algorithm: sort every edge by weight, then add the cycle-free ones. -/\ndef kruskalImpl (g : WeightedGraph) : List MSTEdge :=\n  kruskalLoop g.size\n    ((collectEdges g).mergeSort (fun a b => decide (a.w \u2264 b.w)))\n    (Array.ofFn (n := g.size) fun i => i.val) []\n\n-- !benchmark @end auxcode\n\n-- Main function definition\ndef kruskal_mst (graph : WeightedGraph)\n    (h_precond : kruskal_mst_precond graph) : List MSTEdge :=\n  -- !benchmark @start code\n  kruskalImpl graph\n  -- !benchmark @end code\n\n-- Postcondition definitions\n@[reducible, simp]\ndef kruskal_mst_postcond (graph : WeightedGraph) (result : List MSTEdge)\n    (_ : kruskal_mst_precond graph) : Prop :=\n  -- !benchmark @start postcond\n  is_mst graph result\n  -- !benchmark @end postcond\n\n-- !benchmark @start lemma\n-- !benchmark @end lemma\n\n-- Proof content\ntheorem kruskal_mst_postcond_satisfied (graph : WeightedGraph)\n    (h_precond : kruskal_mst_precond graph) :\n    kruskal_mst_postcond graph (kruskal_mst graph h_precond) h_precond := by\n  -- !benchmark @start proof\n  -- Remaining obligations: edge count, edge validity, spanning connectivity,\n  -- and global optimality against every other spanning tree.\n  refine \u27e8\u27e8?_, ?_, ?_\u27e9, ?_\u27e9\n  -- !benchmark @end proof",
      "comment": "**Not verified.** I did not reach LEAN VERIFIED, and I want to be straight about where things stand rather than imply otherwise.\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe previous session left `Solution.lean` byte-identical to `Original.lean` \u2014 both the `code` and `proof` placeholders were still `sorry`, which is exactly what the independent verifier rejected.\n\n- **`auxcode`** \u2014 genuine, computable Kruskal implementation: `collectEdges` (reads all edges off the adjacency array), `ufFind`/`ufUnion` (union\u2013find, fuel-bounded by the node count so it's structurally terminating), `kruskalLoop` (sweeps the weight-sorted edges, keeping an edge only when `find u \u2260 find v`), and `kruskalImpl` which sorts by weight via `mergeSort` and runs the sweep. This covers TASK.md requirements (1) and (2).\n- **`code`** \u2014 `sorry` replaced by `kruskalImpl graph`. No prohibited placeholder remains here; the verifier's reported blocker is resolved.\n- **`proof`** \u2014 `sorry` replaced by `refine \u27e8\u27e8?_, ?_, ?_\u27e9, ?_\u27e9`, which splits the postcondition into its four obligations.\n\n## Current checker state\n\nThe implementation compiles with no errors (only an `unused variable h_precond` warning). The single remaining error is `unsolved goals` on the theorem, with exactly these four:\n\n1. `(kruskal_mst graph h_precond).length = graph.size - 1`\n2. `\u2200 e \u2208 kruskal_mst graph h_precond, e.is_valid graph`\n3. `is_connected_with_edges graph (kruskal_mst graph h_precond)`\n4. global optimality vs. every other spanning tree\n\nSo the file is strictly better than before \u2014 no `sorry`, working algorithm \u2014 but it does **not** verify.\n\n## Why I stopped\n\nGoals 3 and 4 are a full formalization of Kruskal's correctness against this bespoke encoding (adjacency `Array`, paths as `List Nat` with `getD` indexing). Discharging them honestly needs, at minimum: a bridge between `path_follows_edges` and `Relation.ReflTransGen`/`List.Chain'`; existence of a spanning tree from the directed-path `is_connected` precondition; a union\u2013find invariant proving `find u = find v \u2194` same component; the cut/exchange property for global optimality. Realistically several hundred lines of Mathlib-heavy proof over many compile iterations.\n\nOne structural constraint worth flagging for the next pass: the scaffold fixes `def kruskal_mst` without a `noncomputable` marker, so the returned tree **must be computable**. That rules out the usual shortcut of defining the result by `Classical.choose` over the set of minimum-weight spanning trees \u2014 any optimality argument has to go through a decidable connectivity check (which is, in fairness, exactly verification challenges (1) and (2) in TASK.md).\n\nI used about $0.85 of the $2 budget; the first checker invocation alone cost ~$0.5. With ~$1.15 left I judged that starting the optimality formalization would burn the remainder without converging, so I left the file at a clean handoff point instead. I did not run a semantic audit for sign-off, since the solution isn't complete \u2014 but note nothing here weakens the problem: everything outside the editable regions is untouched, so any proof that eventually compiles is genuinely the stated theorem, including the all-negative-weights case (negative weights change nothing for `is_mst`, since spanning trees all have exactly `size - 1` edges)."
    },
